+#### Storage Options
+
+| Flag | Default | Description |
+|------|---------|-------------|
+| `--object-store <URL>` | none | Keep sedimentree data in an object store instead of `--data-dir`: `s3://bucket/prefix` or `file:///dir`. Also read from `SUBDUCTION_OBJECT_STORE`. |
+
+By default the server stores everything under `--data-dir`. With
+`--object-store`, commits, fragments and blobs live in the bucket and several
+servers can share it; `s3://` takes credentials and endpoint from the `AWS_*`
+environment variables (`AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID`, `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY`,
+`AWS_REGION`, `AWS_ENDPOINT_URL`), which is how S3-compatible services are
+addressed. Keyhive state goes in the same bucket, so `--data-dir` is unused;
+`--admin-addr` is not available.
+
 #### Transport Options
 
 | Flag | Default | Description |
@@ -350,6 +364,26 @@ Caddy automatically handles WebSocket upgrades and TLS certificates.
 
 </details>
 
+## Keyhive Contact Card
+
+With `--auth keyhive` (the default) the server publishes its keyhive contact
+card as JSON on the sync port:
+
+```bash
+curl https://sync.example.com/.well-known/keyhive/contact-card.json
+```
+
+The same card is at `/contact-card.json` and `/contact-card`. A client hands
+it to keyhive to delegate access to the server. With
+`--auth open` the path answers 404.
+
+## Static Files
+
+`--static-dir <DIR>` serves the files in a directory to plain HTTP `GET`
+requests on the sync port, with `/` as `index.html`. WebSocket upgrades and
+long-poll requests are unaffected, so a server can carry a homepage at the
+same URL clients sync with.
+
